---
id: org.dxos.ui.react-ui-canvas
name: SceneEngine
version: 0.1.0
---

The scene engine: an infinite, multi-depth diagram surface. A scene is typed nodes and typed links in
one flat coordinate space; a node of type `scene` is a portal into another scene, which is what depth
means. The engine lives here rather than in a plugin because it is UI with no ECHO dependency —
`plugin-canvas` and `plugin-conductor` are thin variant hosts that supply a store, a registry and a
projection. `docs/DESIGN.md` carries the reasoning and the numbered decisions this document cites;
`docs/MIGRATION.md` the plan that brought canvas-editor and canvas-compute onto it.

The seam that makes one surface serve every host is the **projection** (decision 11): the surface
reads a positioned `Scene` and emits `Intent`s, and never writes a coordinate itself. A projection may
apply, rewrite or reject each intent and then re-emits the scene. Freehand is the identity projection;
constrained, dynamic and compute projections own a model of their own and position the scene from it.

Status 2026-09-22: **implemented** through migration milestone M3. Node types are open (`NodeBase` plus
what a type adds), the editor surface is a superset of `react-ui-canvas-editor` minus auto layout, and
`react-ui-canvas-compute` runs its shapes as node definitions over this engine. M4 (ECHO persistence,
the `layout` intent and the plugin switch) is in flight; what M4 adds is marked below.

## Extensions

| Term        | URI                          |
| ----------- | ---------------------------- |
| `type`      | `org.dxos.mdl.type@1.0`      |
| `component` | `org.dxos.mdl.component@1.0` |
| `op`        | `org.dxos.mdl.op@1.0`        |
| `feat`      | `org.dxos.mdl.feat@1.0`      |
| `scenario`  | `org.dxos.mdl.scenario@1.0`  |
| `rule`      | `org.dxos.mdl.rule@1.0`      |
| `test`      | `org.dxos.mdl.test@1.0`      |

## Types

Effect `Schema` structures in `src/model/types.ts`; plain schemas, so a host wraps them into an ECHO
type without changing a field (decision 3).

```mdl
type Scene
  desc: >
    One diagram: nodes and links keyed by id in a single flat coordinate space (decision 4). Bounds are
    NEVER stored — they are derived from the content, padded and grown to the major grid (decision 6),
    so a scene has no edges to run out of. `createSceneSchema(nodes)` composes the schema from a
    registry's node schemas; `OpenScene` is the same over `NodeBase`, which is what the engine itself
    can validate for a host it does not know the types of.
  fields:
    id: SceneId
    name?: string
    nodes: Record<NodeId, Node>
    links: Record<LinkId, Link>
```

```mdl
type NodeBase
  desc: >
    What every node is, whatever its type adds (decision 1). The frame is `size` centred on `center`;
    everything the engine does — hit testing, ports, resize, marquee, portal mapping — reads only these
    fields, which is why a host type is a first-class citizen rather than a special case. `z` is a
    fractional order key (`utils/order.ts`), shared with links: one namespace, one selection, one undo
    log, one `update` intent for both.
  fields:
    id: NodeId
    type: NodeType                    # 'rect' | 'ellipse' | 'class' | 'note' | 'scene' | a host's own
    z: string                         # fractional order key
    locked?: boolean                  # no intent moves, resizes or deletes it
    center: Point
    size: Size
    ports?: Port[]                    # absent: the type's definition supplies them (decision 12)
    style?: NodeStyle
```

```mdl
type BuiltinNode
  desc: >
    The engine's own five, each `NodeBase` plus its own fields; `isRectNode`-style guards narrow to
    them, sound because the registry maps one type name to one schema. A host's types are `NodeBase`
    to the engine and render through their own `NodeDef.component`.
  literals: RectNode | EllipseNode | ClassNode | TextNode | PortalNode
  fields:
    RectNode: { label?: string }
    EllipseNode: { label?: string }       # inscribed in the frame; one port per side, at its centre
    ClassNode: { name, attributes: string[], methods: string[] }   # UML compartments
    TextNode: { text: string }
    PortalNode: { scene: SceneId }        # renders the referenced scene scaled into this frame
```

```mdl
type NodeStyle
  desc: >
    Presentation a node carries; every field optional, the frame supplying the default look. `hue` is a
    theme hue colouring fill, text and border together (never a raw colour), so a scene stays themed in
    both modes.
  fields:
    hue?: string                      # a theme hue, annotated for the Form's hue picker
    rounded?: boolean
    fill?: boolean
    border?: boolean
    guide?: boolean                   # dashed and unfilled: an annotation, not content
    fontSize?: number                 # text size in the node's own scene units; overrides the view's class
    className?: string                # a host's own look
```

```mdl
type Link
  desc: >
    A typed edge: `line` (straight), `curve` (cubic Bézier leaving each port along its side's normal),
    `spline` (the polyline through its control `points`, each interior point rounded by a quadratic that
    takes the point as its control, so a corner reads as a corner) or `smart` (routed rather than drawn:
    a stub leaves each port along its normal by half a major cell and one segment joins them, rounded the
    same way — nothing stored, so the route follows the nodes). A link is NOT a node with a special
    kind — node-only operations never have to filter links out (decision 9).
  fields:
    id: LinkId
    type: 'line' | 'curve' | 'spline' | 'smart'
    z: string
    locked?: boolean
    source: Endpoint
    target: Endpoint
    directed?: boolean                # shorthand for ends: { end: 'arrow' }
    ends?: { start?: Marker, end?: Marker }    # 'arrow' | 'circle'; replaces what directed implies
    points?: Point[]                  # spline only, in order
```

```mdl
type Endpoint
  desc: >
    Either end of a link, and the reason a link can exist before both its ends do: `{node, port?}` with
    no port pairs automatically (the closest appropriate pair, recomputed on every projection, so a
    moved node re-routes), and `{point}` is a free end at a scene point (decision 3) — what a link tool
    dragged on empty canvas draws, and what dragging an end off a node leaves behind.
  literals: PortEndpoint | PointEndpoint
  fields:
    PortEndpoint: { node: NodeId, port?: PortId }
    PointEndpoint: { point: Point }
```

```mdl
type Port
  desc: >
    An attachment point on a node's frame. Ports come from the type, not the instance (decision 12);
    `portsPerSide` spreads them along each side (never at a corner) and they draw at the major grid line
    nearest each offset, so a link lands on a line rather than near one. `accepts` constrains which end
    of a link may land here, and a link between two constrained ports is directed by that alone.
  fields:
    id: PortId                        # '<side><index>', e.g. 'e2'
    side: 'n' | 'e' | 's' | 'w'
    offset: number                    # 0..1 along that side
    accepts?: 'in' | 'out' | 'any'    # default any
```

```mdl
type Camera
  desc: >
    The view's position over one scene: screen = (scene + {x, y}) * zoom. Per-view state in an atom,
    never in the model (two views of one scene stay independent), clamped to MIN_ZOOM..MAX_ZOOM (1/32,
    32). Coordinates are per-scene and local (decision 5) — a global (x, y, depth) compounds float error
    through the compositor after a few levels.
  fields:
    x: number
    y: number
    zoom: number
```

```mdl
type NodeDef
  desc: >
    A node type's definition: its schema, what it renders, where its ports are, how it may be
    manipulated, and how the palette presents it. Per type, never per instance, so the data stays small
    and ports come for free. A host composes its registry (and with `createSceneSchema` its scene
    schema) from the built-ins and its own — this is the whole of what "open node types" means.
  fields:
    type: NodeType
    name: string
    icon: string
    key?: string                      # palette shortcut; a large registry leaves most without one
    group?: string                    # palette group
    schema: Schema.Codec<NodeBase>
    component: Component<NodeViewProps>
    create: (props: { id, z, center, size }) => Node
    defaultSize: Size
    ports?: (node: Node) => Port[]    # explicit layout; absent, portsPerSide spreads them
    portsPerSide?: number             # default 3; the ellipse takes 1
    resizable?: boolean
    minSize?: Size
    maxSize?: Size
    openable?: boolean                # double-click opens it (a portal drills in)
```

```mdl
type SceneStore
  desc: >
    Scenes by id behind atoms, so a view subscribes to exactly the scene it shows. The seam that lets
    persistence change without the surface noticing: `createMemoryStore` for stories and tests,
    `createEchoStore(board)` over `CanvasBoard.layout` for a plugin (M4).
  fields:
    scenes: Atom.Writable<Record<SceneId, Scene>>
    scene: (id: SceneId) => Atom<Scene | undefined>
```

```mdl
type Projection
  desc: >
    The only thing the surface knows about the model (decision 11). It reads a positioned scene and
    receives intents; what an intent MEANS is the projection's business — freehand writes the
    coordinates, constrained rewrites them as cardinal constraints, dynamic keeps a graph and positions
    rows from it, compute mirrors creates and links into a compute graph. `snapshot` is opaque and
    identical (`===`) when nothing changed, which is how the view keeps an undo log without knowing what
    the model is.
  fields:
    scene: Atom<Scene>
    apply: (intent: Intent) => void
    capabilities: Capabilities        # move, resize, link, create, delete, update — each optional
    snapshot: () => unknown
    restore: (snapshot: unknown) => void
```

```mdl
type Tool
  desc: The active tool; a node type draws that node, a link type is what a port drag creates.
  literals: { kind: 'select' } | { kind: 'hand' } | { kind: 'node', type } | { kind: 'link', type }
```

## Components

One folder per component under `src/components`, stories beside it, all behind the `src/scene.ts`
barrel. Rendering is DOM with the camera as one CSS transform, SVG for the link and control layers
(decision 7); no third-party canvas engine, and d3 only as pure functions (decision 8).

```mdl
component SceneView
  desc: >
    The surface: one scene under a camera, with the palette, toolbar, breadcrumbs and control frame
    around it. Owns the pointer state machine, the key table, navigation between depths and the undo
    log; owns NO model — every change leaves through `projection.apply`.
  props:
    store: SceneStore
    root: SceneId
    nodes?: NodeRegistry              # defaults to the built-in five
    links?: LinkRegistry
    createProjection?: (options) => Projection     # per scene; freehand by default
    projection?: Projection           # a host-owned one instead, shared with its panels
    atoms?: SceneViewAtoms            # externally owned view state (two views, a persisted camera)
    grid?: number                     # minor spacing in scene px (16)
    liveDepth?: number                # nested levels that may mount live (decision 10)
    showPalette?: boolean
    showToolbar?: boolean
    readonly?: boolean
    overlay?: ReactNode               # extra layers in scene coordinates (e.g. compute's Bullets)
  state:
    camera: Camera
    path: SceneId[]                   # root to the scene being edited; its length is the depth
    selection: Set<ElementId>         # nodes and links together
    hover, point, tool, linkType, snap, debug, drag, editing, history, undo, clipboard
  layout: |
    ┌─ scene-view ────────────────────────────────┐
    │ [toolbar]                       [breadcrumbs]│
    │ [palette]                                    │
    │        grid (screen space, camera-fed)       │
    │        scene layer (one CSS transform)       │
    │        link layer (svg, same transform)      │
    │        control frame (svg, same transform)   │
    │        overlay (host's, same transform)      │
    └──────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

```mdl
component SceneLayer
  desc: >
    The nodes and links of one scene, and — for a portal node — the child scene rendered live inside it
    at the portal's scale, which is what makes depth continuous rather than a page change. Below
    `liveDepth` a portal draws a preview instead; a portal being drilled into renders plain, as the
    child will look once entered.
  props:
    scene: Scene
    registry: NodeRegistry
    zoom: number                      # camera zoom × the portal scales above it
    depth: number
    liveDepth: number
```

```mdl
component ControlFrame
  desc: >
    Everything drawn about the selection rather than in the scene: the selection outline, eight resize
    handles, the hover border, ports, the rubber band of a link drag, the marquee, a spline's control
    points and the midpoint dots that add one, and the provisional frame a create gesture will land in.
    Handles draw above ports, so a handle wins at a side centre.
  props:
    scene, selection, hover, drag, zoom
    showPorts: boolean                # every node (a link tool) or only the hovered one
    createFrame?: Bounds
  emits:
    onHandlePointerDown, onPortPointerDown, onEndPointerDown, onPointPointerDown
```

```mdl
component Palette
  desc: Node and link types from the registry, grouped, each with its shortcut; entries are pdnd drag sources.
  props: { tool, nodes: NodeRegistry, links: LinkRegistry, capabilities }
```

```mdl
component Toolbar
  desc: >
    Optional chrome (decision 5): up and breadcrumbs, fit, zoom, snap, undo/redo, cut/copy/paste/delete,
    a create menu, auto layout, debug, and a readout of zoom, pointer and depth. Every label prints its
    chord from `model/keys.ts` rather than naming one.
  props: { actions, nodes, capabilities, snap, debug }
```

```mdl
component Properties
  desc: >
    The schema-driven `Form` over the selected node or link's OWN schema — the registry already carries
    it, so a host type gets a properties panel with no extra work. Edits leave as `update` intents;
    `readonly` shows the fields without letting them change. Two field renderers are the canvas's own:
    a UML compartment edits as one entry per line, and `center` / `size` edit as `GeometryField`.
  props: { projection, atoms, nodes?, fields?, grid?, readonly? }
```

```mdl
component GeometryField
  desc: >
    A node's `center` or `size` as one row of two labelled number cells (X / Y, W / H) rather than a
    collapsible fieldset. It belongs to the canvas rather than `react-ui-form` because it answers to the
    grid: an arrow steps a minor cell and Shift a major one — the units an arrow nudge moves a selected
    node by — and a committed value snaps while snapping is on, so typing a number leaves a node as
    snapped as dragging it. A step is a complete gesture like a toggle and saves at once; typing digits
    waits for blur, so a half-written number never reaches the model.
  props: { grid?, snap? }
```

```mdl
component Breadcrumbs
  desc: The path from the root to the scene being edited; a click drills out to that level.
  props: { path: SceneId[], store }
```

```mdl
component Grid
  desc: >
    Multi-resolution grid in screen space, fed the camera. Levels are a fourfold apart, from a quarter of
    the minor grid to far past the major one, and a level's line weight follows its ON-SCREEN spacing
    rather than its index among the drawn levels — so the same lines are drawn at the same weight either
    side of a drill-in, which is what makes the swap invisible.
  props: { camera, size }
```

```mdl
component PartEditor
  desc: >
    In-place text editing: a `react-ui-editor` over one `TextPart` of a node (a rect or ellipse label, a
    text body, a class's name, attributes or methods). Enter commits an `update` (Mod-Enter in
    multi-line parts), Escape rejects, blur commits.
  props: { node, part, bounds, onCommit, onCancel }
```

## Operations

The projection seam IS the operation surface: these are the only ways the model changes, and every one
of them is a value the view hands to `projection.apply`. `reduceIntent` is freehand's semantics as a
pure reducer, which is what makes them testable without a DOM.

```mdl
op move
  desc: Translate every unlocked node in `ids` by `delta`. The gesture snaps to the MINOR grid as it runs.
  input:
    ids: NodeId[]
    delta: Point
  output: Scene
```

```mdl
op resize
  desc: >
    Set one unlocked node's frame. Shift keeps the centre, otherwise the opposite edge is fixed;
    `minSize` and `maxSize` come from the type's definition (`utils/resize.ts`).
  input:
    id: NodeId
    bounds: Bounds
  output: Scene
```

```mdl
op link
  desc: >
    Add a link. Rejected when a node end names a node that does not exist, or when both ends are the
    same node; free ends need nothing, which is why a link tool works on empty canvas.
  input:
    link: Link
  output: Scene
  errors:
    unknownNode: an end names a node not in the scene — the intent is dropped, not partially applied
    selfLink: both ends are the same node
```

```mdl
op create
  desc: Add a node, built by its type's `NodeDef.create`. A new node arrives on the MAJOR grid.
  input:
    node: Node
  output: Scene
```

```mdl
op delete
  desc: >
    Remove nodes and links by id. A link loses its meaning with either end, so deleting a node deletes
    its links too — the projection does this, never the view.
  input:
    ids: ElementId[]
  output: Scene
```

```mdl
op reorder
  desc: Set one element's fractional `z`; nodes and links share the namespace and the key space.
  input:
    id: ElementId
    z: string
  output: Scene
```

```mdl
op update
  desc: >
    Property edits — a label, a text body, geometry, a spline's control points, a style. `id` and `type`
    are never changed by an update, whatever the values carry.
  input:
    id: ElementId
    values: NodeValues | LinkValues
  output: Scene
```

```mdl
op batch
  desc: Several intents as ONE model change, so a paste or a drill-in fix-up is one undo step.
  input:
    intents: Intent[]
  output: Scene
```

```mdl
op layout
  desc: >
    Arrange the scene into ranked rows along its links, through `@dxos/diagram`'s ranking — the engine
    the dynamic projection already lays its graph out with, rather than a second one. Unlike the old
    editor's `@antv` layout it keeps every node: type, size and content are untouched and only the
    centre moves. An intent rather than a view action, so a projection that owns its own positions may
    rewrite or refuse it, and a read-only view cannot invoke it.
  input:
    ids?: ElementId[]                 # absent: the whole scene
  output: Scene
  note: >
    A row is as tall as its tallest node and a column as wide as its widest, so mixed sizes do not
    overlap; every centre lands on the major grid; a locked node stays where it is. The toolbar's
    button is enabled by `Capabilities.layout` and lays out the selection when two or more are
    selected, otherwise the board.
```

## Features

```mdl
feat F-1: Depth and navigation
  Scene nesting IS the hierarchy (decision 4); a portal node renders its child live, so moving between
  levels is a camera move rather than a page change.
  req F-1.1: Double-clicking a portal drills in; Escape drills out; the breadcrumbs drill to any level.
  req F-1.2: Drill-in stops where the child would pass 1:1, so its text lands at natural size rather
    than magnified to fill the view.
  req F-1.3: Zooming past a portal drills in on its own; zooming out below the arrival coverage drills
    out. Auto-exit measures against the coverage the view ARRIVED with, never an absolute threshold, so
    a capped child (or a frame that shrinks as its first node is drawn) is not thrown straight back out.
  req F-1.4: A portal's frame is the portal box times a power of the grid ratio, never once, placed on
    the major grid scaled by that factor: the child's grid lines land on the parent's own lines, not
    merely at their spacing, so the grids stay aligned through a drill-in.
  req F-1.5: A scene entered through a portal keeps the frame it arrived with for the visit and exits
    through that same frame; the root's bounds stay derived.
  req F-1.6: At most `liveDepth` levels mount live; deeper portals draw as previews (decision 10).
  req F-1.7: Alt+Left / Alt+Right walk the view's own camera history.
```

```mdl
feat F-2: Grid and snapping
  req F-2.1: The grid is drawn only while snapping is on (G toggles both): its lines are what a gesture
    lands on, so drawing them when nothing snaps states a constraint the canvas is not applying.
  req F-2.2: Gestures snap to the FINEST LEVEL THE GRID ACTUALLY DRAWS, read back through the same rule
    the component filters by, so the lines and the snap cannot part company. A unit fixed in scene units
    diverges as soon as the zoom moves: far enough in the drawn lines are finer than the snap, and in a
    nested scene — entered at a fraction of the parent's zoom — they are coarser and the snap lands on
    nothing visible.
  req F-2.3: A created node arrives on the major level; a move, a resize or a dragged control point
    snaps to the minor one, so placement is not coarse while a node's size and ports stay aligned.
  req F-2.4: An arrow nudges by the minor grid, Shift+arrow by a major cell.
  req F-2.5: A link band follows the pointer exactly and snaps once, as it lands.
  req F-2.6: Snapping off means every gesture is free and the grid is hidden; nothing else changes.
```

```mdl
feat F-3: Selection and editing
  req F-3.1: Nodes and links are selected together in one set; Delete removes either.
  req F-3.2: A marquee replaces the selection, Shift adds, Alt subtracts.
  req F-3.3: A port drag creates a link; dropping on empty canvas leaves a free end; dragging an end
    re-attaches it, previewed as created while hovering a valid drop.
  req F-3.4: A selected spline offers the midpoint of every span; pressing one inserts a control point
    there and moves it in the same gesture.
  req F-3.5: A create drag previews the bounds the node will land in; a palette drop shows that frame
    alone, since the pointer already carries the drag's own preview.
  req F-3.6: A box drawn on the canvas is exactly what the pointer swept — no minimum and no fallback to
    the type's default — and a gesture that snapped to nothing creates nothing rather than planting a
    default-sized node under the click.
  req F-3.7: A create with NO drawn box (a palette drop, the toolbar's create menu) takes the type's
    default size divided by the camera zoom, so it covers the same screen area at any zoom and any
    depth. The camera is the right quantity because it is independent of content: the portal's own
    factor grows with the child's bounds, so scaling by it would feed back, each node enlarging the next.
  req F-3.8: Picking a shape or link tool clears the selection; the node the gesture makes becomes it.
  req F-3.9: Double-click edits a node's text part in place; Enter commits, Escape rejects.
  req F-3.10: The properties panel edits the selected element's own schema through `update` intents,
    `style.fontSize` among them: set, it overrides the view's own size class for every text part.
```

```mdl
feat F-4: Undo and clipboard
  req F-4.1: Undo is a snapshot log over the projection seam, so it works for any projection without
    the view knowing what the model is.
  req F-4.2: A restored snapshot reconciles the projection's own model (the compute projection takes
    its nodes and edges along).
  req F-4.3: Cut / copy / paste use a per-view clipboard: fresh ids, links rewired between the pasted
    nodes only, applied as one `batch`.
```

```mdl
feat F-5: Read-only
  req F-5.1: `readonly` lets the view select, pan, zoom and drill, and nothing else.
  req F-5.2: No handle, port, tool or toolbar edit is drawn or reachable, whatever the projection's
    capabilities would allow — the stricter of the two wins.
```

```mdl
feat F-6: Open registry
  req F-6.1: The engine operates on `NodeBase`; a host's type is not a special case.
  req F-6.2: A host composes its registry from the built-ins and its own defs, and its scene schema with
    `createSceneSchema`.
  req F-6.3: An unknown type still renders (`UnknownNodeView`) rather than throwing: a scene written by
    a newer host opens.
  req F-6.4: Ports come from the definition; a node may still carry its own.
```

```mdl
feat F-7: Persistence (M4)
  req F-7.1: `createEchoStore(board)` maps nodes to `CanvasBoard.layout.nodes` and links to
    `layout.edges`, deriving `z` from array order on first read, so every board written by the old
    editor opens unchanged.
  req F-7.2: The store is the only seam that changes: `SceneView` takes the ECHO store exactly as it
    takes the memory one.
  req F-7.3: `plugin-conductor`'s `CanvasArticle` renders `SceneView` with the compute projection over
    that store.
```

```mdl
feat F-8: Links between shapes
  req F-8.1: With a link tool a node's BODY is a source like a port is, so two shapes can be joined
    without aiming at their dots; the endpoint carries no port and routing picks the side.
  req F-8.2: A drop resolves the same way: a port claims it only while the pointer is within reach,
    anywhere else on the node binds to the body, and a node whose ports all refuse the end takes a body
    link rather than refusing the drop.
  req F-8.3: Ports are drawn for the node under the pointer alone (and a drop target mid-drag, which
    pointer capture hides from hover) — every node's ports at once is a field of dots over the diagram.
  req F-8.4: A `smart` link is routed, not drawn: a stub leaves each port along its side's normal by
    half a major cell and one segment joins them. Nothing is stored, so it follows the nodes; two ports
    on the same side double back through the middle rather than cutting across their own nodes.
```

## Rules

Conventions this engine's code must hold that a linter cannot express; each block is discovered by the
agentic-review harness.

```mdl
rule intents-not-coordinates: The surface emits intents; only a projection writes coordinates
  Every model change leaves the view through `projection.apply(intent)`. A component under
  `src/components` must not write `center`, `size`, `z`, `points` or an endpoint into a scene, a
  store or a node object — not through `updateScene`, not by constructing a `Scene` and putting it
  back, not by mutating a node it read from the projection. Transient gesture state (the `drag`
  atom's `delta`, `bounds`, `points`, a preview node or link that is never applied) is exactly what
  that state is for and is not a violation. Flag a component that reaches `store`/`updateScene`/
  `putScene` to change geometry, or that calls `reduceIntent` itself instead of applying an intent.
  Do not flag projections (`src/model/projections/**`, `src/model/projection.ts`), stores, or a host's
  own projection outside this package.
  scope: dir
  files:
    - src/components/**/*.tsx
    - src/components/**/*.ts
  grep: updateScene|putScene|reduceIntent
  severity: error
```

```mdl
rule key-chords-live-in-the-table: Every chord is a KEY_BINDINGS entry, matched and labelled from it
  A keyboard chord the engine honours is declared in `src/model/keys.ts` and matched with
  `keyAction` / `matchesBinding`; a label or tooltip naming a shortcut prints it with `shortcutFor`.
  Flag a handler comparing `event.key` to a literal, or a UI string containing a chord written out
  ('⌘Z', 'Ctrl+C', 'Press G'), anywhere under `src/components`. Tool letters are the one exception:
  they live on the registry entries as `NodeDef.key` / `LinkDef.key` and are matched by the palette
  through `isToolKey`. Do not flag `keys.ts` itself or its test.
  scope: dir
  files:
    - src/components/**/*.tsx
    - src/components/**/*.ts
  grep: event\.key|\.key ===|⌘|Ctrl\+|Cmd\+
  severity: error
```

```mdl
rule named-react-imports: Import React members by name, never through a `React.` namespace
  Use `import { useMemo, type Ref } from 'react'` and call `useMemo(...)`; do not write
  `React.useMemo`, `React.FC`, `React.ReactNode` or `import React from 'react'`. A forwarded ref is
  named `forwardedRef`. Flag any `React.` member access and any default import of react in this
  package's source. Do not flag `import type * as React from 'react'` used solely in a `.d.ts`
  ambient declaration, nor the archived canvas under `src/archive`.
  scope: dir
  files:
    - src/**/*.tsx
    - src/**/*.ts
  grep: React\.|import React
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule scene-bounds-are-derived: Never store a scene's bounds
  A scene's extent is the union of its node frames, padded and grown to the major grid, computed where
  it is needed (`utils/hit.ts`, `sceneBounds`). Do not add a bounds, extent, width or height field to
  `Scene`, cache one on a store, or persist one — a stored extent is a second source of truth that
  goes stale on every move and gives an infinite canvas an edge (decision 6). Flag a `Scene` literal,
  schema or ECHO record carrying such a field. A `Bounds` VALUE passed around (a portal frame, a
  marquee, a resize) is not a stored bound and is not a violation.
  scope: dir
  files:
    - src/model/**/*.ts
    - src/utils/**/*.ts
  severity: error
```

## Acceptance

```mdl
scenario T-1: Drill in and back out
  given: the Nested story, a portal visible in the root scene
  when: double-clicking the portal, then pressing Escape
  then:
    - the camera animates into the portal and stops at or before 1:1 for the child's content
    - the grid lines either side of the swap are drawn at the same spacing and weight
    - the breadcrumbs gain a level, then lose it again; the camera returns to where it left
```

```mdl
scenario T-2: A link survives its node moving
  given: two nodes joined by a curve with no explicit ports
  when: dragging one node across the scene
  then: the link re-pairs to the closest appropriate ports on every frame, and still leaves each port
    along its side's normal
```

```mdl
scenario T-3: A free-ended link
  given: the Freehand story with the line tool active
  when: dragging on empty canvas, away from every node
  then: a link with two free `{point}` ends is created; dragging one end onto a node's port attaches it
```

```mdl
scenario T-4: Read-only
  given: the Readonly story
  when: clicking a node, then attempting to drag it, resize it, or press Delete
  then: the node is selected, no handle or port is drawn, and the scene is unchanged
```

```mdl
scenario T-5: Undo across a projection
  given: the Constrained story
  when: moving a node (which the projection rewrites into constraints), then pressing ⌘Z
  then: both the positioned scene and the constraint list return to their previous state in one step
```

```mdl
scenario T-6: An existing board opens unchanged (M4)
  given: a `CanvasBoard` written by `react-ui-canvas-editor`, with nodes and edges in `layout`
  when: opening it in the plugin on the scene engine
  then: every node keeps its position and size, every edge its ends, and the z-order matches the array
    order it was stored in
```

## QA

Executable tests over the **storybook**, not the app: the engine ships no plugin of its own, and the
stories are the surface a host composes. Run `moon run storybook-react:serve` (9009) or the e2e
storybook (9006); `moon run react-ui-canvas:e2e` runs the automated subset.

```mdl
test QA-1: The editor surface end to end
  desc: |
    The gestures a host inherits by rendering `SceneView`. Ten of these steps are automated in
    `src/playwright/scene.spec.ts` over the Freehand and Readonly stories; the rest are human because
    they judge motion (the drill-in animation, grid continuity) rather than a DOM state.
  actors: human, agent
  covers: [F-1.1, F-1.2, F-2.1, F-2.2, F-3.1, F-3.2, F-3.3, F-5.1, T-1, T-3, T-4]
  status: partially-automated
  given:
    - a storybook serving `ui/react-ui-canvas/scene/SceneView`
  steps:
    - name: Open Freehand
      do: Open the Freehand story at 1400×800.
      expect: four nodes and three links, fitted; the toolbar reads a zoom, a pointer and depth 0
    - name: Marquee
      do: Drag on empty canvas across two nodes, then repeat holding Alt.
      expect: the first drag selects both; the Alt drag removes them from the selection
    - name: Move and snap
      do: Drag a node with snap on (G toggles it).
      expect: the node's edges land on minor grid lines; the readout follows the pointer
    - name: Draw a link
      do: Hover a node, drag from one of its ports onto another node.
      expect: a band follows the pointer, the target's ports show, and a link is created on release
    - name: Drill in
      do: Switch to the Nested story and double-click a portal.
      expect: the camera animates in, stops at natural text size, and the grid does not jump at the swap
    - name: Drill out
      do: Press Escape.
      expect: the camera returns to the parent, the breadcrumbs lose a level
    - name: Read-only
      do: Open the Readonly story and try to drag a node.
      expect: it selects, draws no handles, and does not move
  after:
    - name: None
      do: Stories hold no persistent state; reload to reset.
      expect: the fixture is back
```

```mdl
test QA-2: Persistence round trip (M4)
  desc: |
    The one thing stories cannot cover, because it needs a space: that a board written by the old
    editor opens unchanged on the engine, and that an edit made on the engine survives a reload.
  actors: human
  covers: [F-7.1, F-7.2, F-7.3, T-6]
  status: unverified
  given:
    - an identity and an open space
    - a CanvasBoard created by the previous editor, with at least two nodes and one edge
  steps:
    - name: Open it
      do: Open the board in Composer.
      expect: every node is where it was, every edge joins the same two nodes
    - name: Edit
      do: Move a node, add one, and join them.
      expect: the changes appear immediately
    - name: Reload
      do: Reload the app and reopen the board.
      expect: the move, the new node and the new link are all still there
```

---

## Appendix: Extension Definitions

```mdl
ext type
  uri: org.dxos.mdl.type@1.0
  desc: A named data structure with typed fields and optional literals.
  fields:
    desc?: Prose
    fields?: FieldMap
    literals?: UnionList
    extends?: TypeRef[]
```

```mdl
ext component
  uri: org.dxos.mdl.component@1.0
  desc: A UI component with props, internal state, slots, actions, and events.
  fields:
    desc?: Prose
    props?: FieldMap
    state?: FieldMap
    slots?: FieldMap
    actions?: ActionMap
    emits?: EventMap
    layout?: CodeBlock
```

```mdl
ext op
  uri: org.dxos.mdl.op@1.0
  desc: |
    A named operation with typed inputs, outputs, and declared errors.
    Pure ops have no effects or requires. Effectful ops declare both.
  fields:
    desc?: Prose
    input?: FieldMap
    output?: TypeExpr
    errors?: ErrorMap
    effects?: EffectList
    requires?: ServiceList
    note?: Prose
```

```mdl
ext feat
  uri: org.dxos.mdl.feat@1.0
  desc: A named feature grouping one or more requirements.
  fields:
    desc?: Prose
    req: RequirementList
  nesting: self
  inline-prose: true
```

```mdl
ext scenario
  uri: org.dxos.mdl.scenario@1.0
  desc: An acceptance scenario expressed as given / when / then steps.
  fields:
    given?: Step | Step[]
    when?: Step | Step[]
    then: Step | Step[]
    tags?: TagList
```

```mdl
ext rule
  uri: org.dxos.mdl.rule@1.0
  desc: A machine-checked review rule applied by an LLM subagent to changed files.
  inline-prose: true
  fields:
    files: Glob | Glob[]              # paths this rule reviews, relative to `scope`
    grep?: Prose                      # optional regex pre-filter over file contents
    severity?: warn | error           # defaults to warn
    scope?: dir | repo                # glob base: this file's dir (default) or repo root
```

```mdl
ext test
  uri: org.dxos.mdl.test@1.0
  desc: |
    An executable QA test: ordered steps, each with a human instruction (`do`) and, where the step maps
    to an invocable operation, an agent binding (`invoke`).
  fields:
    desc?: Prose
    actors?: TagList                  # human | agent
    covers?: TagList                  # feat req and scenario ids
    status?: unverified | partially-automated | verified
    given?: ProseList
    steps: StepList
    after?: StepList
```
